{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel ate the manna forty years.</b> As it appears, they were not forty complete years, for one month was lacking from them, since the manna began to fall on the first day of the week, and it was the first of Iyyar, and the dough that they brought out of Egypt sufficed them for one month. If so, the manna ought to have ceased on the first of Iyyar, but Scripture testifies that it ceased on the first of Nisan, when Israel was in the plains of Jericho. And it is written (Joshua 5): ״ויאכלו מעבור הארץ ממחרת הפסח מצות וקלוי וישבות המן ממחרת״ — and they ate of the produce of the land on the day after the Passover, unleavened cakes and parched grain, and the manna ceased on the day after. Therefore one must say that the sense of 'forty years' is close to forty years, and so that you not understand them to have been forty complete, for this reason it was necessary to repeat without qualification: ״את המן אכלו עד באם אל קצה ארץ כנען״ — the manna they ate until they came to the border of the land of Canaan — for thus you find written: ״וישבת המן ממחרת באכלם מעבור הארץ ולא היה עוד לבני ישראל מן ויאכלו מתבואת ארץ כנען בשנה ההיא״ — and the manna ceased on the day after, when they ate of the produce of the land, and the children of Israel had manna no more, and they ate of the produce of the land of Canaan that year. And we learn that for Israel, for whom the manna fell all the days of Moses, it still fell for them after the death of Moses, while they were under the authority of Joshua, for thirty-eight days, for Moses died on the seventh of Adar, and all Israel wept for him thirty days, and they crossed the Jordan on the tenth of the first month, for thus it is explicit in Joshua, and the manna did not cease until the fifteenth of Nisan. And when you count from the seventh of Adar, when Moses died, until the fifteenth of Nisan, when the manna ceased, you find thirty-eight days." "<b>But</b> Rashi explained that they were forty complete years, and wrote on this that on the seventh of Adar, when Moses died, the manna ceased, and even so they were forty complete years, for two reasons. The first: the cakes (עוגות) that they brought out of Egypt — they tasted in them the taste of manna, and if so, one month would not be lacking. The second: that when the manna ceased on the seventh of Adar, when Moses died, the manna that had fallen the day before sufficed them until they brought the omer on the sixteenth of Nisan. And in all this is fulfilled: ״את המן אכלו ארבעים שנה״ — the manna they ate forty years — complete."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The verse cited from Joshua 5: ״וישבת המן ממחרת באכלם מעבור הארץ ולא היה עוד לבני ישראל מן ויאכלו מתבואת ארץ כנען בשנה ההיא״ conflates Joshua 5:11–12; the wording follows the commentator's composite citation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:22.743529935Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}